/* data/learn-c/cs-12-03.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/cs-12-03.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["cs-12-03"] = {"1":{"s":"C++ is a general purpose language made by Bjarne Stroustrup as an extension of C, with classes and objects. A program has header files, then main(). Tokens are keywords, identifiers, constants, operators and separators. Data types are int, char, float, double, bool and void; cin takes input with the extraction operator and cout gives output with the insertion operator. It has if, switch, for, while and do-while, functions, inline functions, overloading and default arguments.","w":["An identifier starts with a letter or underscore, never a digit, and cannot be a keyword.","cout uses the insertion operator (two less-than signs); cin uses the extraction operator (two greater-than signs)."],"x":["Every C++ program starts with the preprocessor line that includes a header file such as iostream, then the function main(), where execution begins. Statements end with a semicolon and blocks are in curly brackets.","Tokens are the smallest parts of a program: keywords (int, if, while), identifiers (names you choose), constants, operators and punctuation. Variables have a data type: int for whole numbers, float and double for decimals, char for one character, bool for true or false. A const variable cannot change. A reference variable is a second name for a variable.","Input uses cin with the extraction operator, output uses cout with the insertion operator; manipulators like endl and setw format the output. Decisions use if, if else and switch; loops use for, while and do-while, with break and continue.","Functions have a prototype (declaration before use). An inline function is expanded at the place of the call to save the call time. Function overloading is using one name for several functions that differ in the number or type of arguments. A default argument is used when the caller leaves the argument out. The scope resolution operator (two colons) reaches a global variable hidden by a local one."],"e":"A program to add two numbers: include iostream, write main, declare int a, b, sum, read them with cin, set sum = a + b, and show it with cout."},"2":{"s":"An array stores elements of one type in consecutive locations, reached by an index that starts at 0. A pointer holds the address of a variable: declared with a star, given an address with the ampersand. The name of an array is the address of its first element. Call by value passes a copy; call by reference passes the address or a reference so the function changes the original. A string is a character array ending with the null character.","w":["Array indexes start at 0; the last element of an array of size n is n minus 1.","A string of 7 letters needs 8 places in memory, because of the null character."],"x":["An array is declared as type name[size]. The elements are numbered from 0. An array can be given values when it is declared. To pass an array to a function, give its name; the function receives the address of the first element, so it works on the original array, usually with the size as a second argument.","A pointer is a variable that stores a memory address. It is declared with a star, for example int *p; the address of a variable is taken with the ampersand, p = &x; and the value at the address is reached with the star, *p. Pointer arithmetic: adding 1 moves the pointer to the next element; two pointers of the same type can be subtracted but not added. The name of an array is a constant pointer to its first element, so a[i] is the same as *(a + i).","In call by value the function gets a copy of the argument, so the original does not change. In call by reference the function gets the address (a pointer) or a reference, so it works on the original. A string is a character array that ends with the null character, so Prakash (7 letters) needs 8 places. Library functions such as strlen, strcpy and strcat are in the string header file."],"e":"A swap function must use call by reference. void swap(int &a, int &b) changes the two numbers in the caller; if the parameters are ordinary ints, the swap happens only inside the function."},"3":{"s":"Object oriented programming (OOP) builds a program from objects that keep data and the functions that work on the data together. The main ideas are class and object, data abstraction (show only what is needed), encapsulation (bind data and functions in one unit), data hiding (keep the data private), inheritance (a new class takes the properties of an old one) and polymorphism (one name, many forms).","w":["Data hiding means the data is private; the data is reached only through the public functions.","Abstraction is about showing less; encapsulation is about binding together. Do not swap the two."],"x":["In procedural programming the program is a list of functions and the data is separate. In object oriented programming the data and the functions that use it are put together into objects. The program is made bottom-up from objects, and it is easier to extend and to reuse.","The main principles: a class is the plan (type) and an object is a real item made from it. Data abstraction shows only the essential features and hides the details. Encapsulation binds the data and functions into one unit, the class. Data hiding keeps the data private so that outside functions cannot change it by accident. Inheritance gives a new class the properties of an existing class. Polymorphism means one function name or operator doing different jobs.","Benefits: reuse of code through inheritance, safe data through hiding, programs that are easy to change, and a model close to real things."],"e":"A Student class holds roll number and marks as private data, with public functions to read and show them. Outside code cannot set the marks to a wrong value directly."},"4":{"s":"A class is a user-defined type that groups data members and member functions; members are private unless made public. An object is a variable of the class type, made when needed; it takes memory, and its members are used with the dot operator. Member functions can be defined inside the class or outside it with the scope resolution operator. An array of objects holds many objects of one class. A friend function can use the private members of a class.","w":["A class takes no memory until an object is made.","A friend function is not a member of the class, but it can reach the private members, and it is called without the dot operator."],"x":["A class is written with the keyword class, a name, and a body in curly brackets that ends with a semicolon. Data members hold the data and member functions work on it. The private part is hidden from outside; the public part is the interface.","An object is made like a variable: Student s1, s2; Each object has its own copy of the data members, but the member functions are shared. A member is reached with the dot operator, s1.show(). A member function can be defined inside the class (then it is inline) or outside with the class name and the scope resolution operator: void Student::show() { }.","An array of objects is declared like any array: Student s[10]; and each element is used as s[i].getdata(). A friend function is declared inside the class with the keyword friend; it is not a member, but it may use private data. It is useful to work on two classes together, or for operator overloading."],"e":"class Rect with private length and breadth and public set() and area(). Rect r; r.set(4, 5); then showing r.area() with cout gives 20."},"5":{"s":"A constructor is a special member function with the same name as the class and no return type; it runs automatically when an object is made and gives starting values. It can have arguments and can be overloaded. A default constructor has no arguments; a copy constructor makes an object from another object. A destructor has the class name with a tilde, no arguments and no return type; it runs when the object ends.","w":["A constructor has no return type, not even void.","A class can have many constructors (overloading) but only one destructor."],"x":["A constructor is called by itself when an object is created. It has the same name as the class, has no return type, is usually public, and is used to give starting values to the data members. It may take arguments, and a class may have several constructors with different arguments (constructor overloading). A constructor with no arguments is the default constructor.","Dynamic initialization of an object means that the values come at run time: the program reads the values and passes them to a parameterised constructor, for example Rectangle r(a, b). A copy constructor makes a new object from an existing one: ClassName(ClassName &obj). It is used when an object is made from another object, when an object is passed by value, and when it is returned by value.","A destructor is called by itself when an object goes out of scope or is deleted. Its name is the class name with a tilde, such as ~Student(); it has no arguments and no return type and cannot be overloaded. It is used to free memory or other resources that the object took."],"e":"A class with a pointer to dynamic memory takes the memory in the constructor with new and frees it in the destructor with delete, so no memory is lost when the object ends."},"6":{"s":"Operator overloading gives an operator an extra meaning for the objects of a class, using an operator function written with the keyword operator. Operators that cannot be overloaded: the scope resolution, the dot, the dot with star, the conditional and sizeof. Type conversion changes a value from one type to another: basic to class (with a one-argument constructor), class to basic (with an operator function), and class to class.","w":["At least one operand of an overloaded operator must be an object of a class.","Basic to class conversion uses a constructor; class to basic uses a conversion function written with operator."],"x":["With operator overloading the operators can work on objects. The operator function is a member function: return_type operator op (arguments). A unary operator takes no argument as a member function and a binary operator takes one (the right operand). For example c3 = c1 + c2 is turned by the compiler into c3 = c1.operator+(c2).","Rules: only existing operators can be overloaded; ::, the dot, the dot with star, ?: and sizeof cannot; the number of operands and the precedence do not change; and the meaning for the built-in types does not change.","Type conversion: the compiler converts basic types by itself (implicit), or the programmer uses a cast (explicit). For user types: basic to class needs a constructor that takes the basic type; class to basic needs a conversion function, operator int(), with no return type written and no arguments; class to class is done with a conversion function in the source class or a constructor in the destination class."],"e":"Time t = 135; calls the constructor Time(int) to make 2 hours 15 minutes from 135 minutes (basic to class). int m = t; uses operator int() to give 135 back (class to basic)."},"7":{"s":"Inheritance makes a new derived class from an existing base class, so the derived class gets the members of the base and can add its own, which gives reuse of code. Syntax: class Derived : public Base. Types: single (one base), multilevel (a chain), multiple (more than one base), hierarchical (many derived classes from one base) and hybrid. A virtual base class keeps one shared copy when a class gets the same base through two paths.","w":["Multiple inheritance means one derived class with several base classes; multilevel is a chain.","A virtual base class stops two copies of the same base in hybrid inheritance."],"x":["In inheritance the new class is the derived class and the old one is the base class. The derived class gets the members of the base class and may add more, so the old code is reused and need not be written again. It saves time and mistakes. Syntax: class Derived : public Base { };","Single inheritance: one base and one derived class. Multilevel: A is the base of B and B is the base of C. Multiple: one derived class with two or more bases, class C : public A, public B. Hierarchical: several classes are derived from one base. Hybrid: a mix of these.","In hybrid inheritance a class may get the same base class by two paths, which gives two copies of its members and causes confusion. Declaring the base class as virtual, class B : virtual public A, makes the derived classes share one copy."],"e":"A Person base class has name and age. Student is derived from Person and adds roll number, so a Student object has name, age and roll number without repeating the Person code."},"8":{"s":"Polymorphism means one name with many forms. At compile time it is function and operator overloading (early binding). At run time it is by virtual functions (late binding): a base class function declared with virtual is redefined in the derived class, and a call through a base class pointer runs the function of the real object. A pure virtual function has no body and is written with equals zero; a class with one is abstract.","w":["Early binding is at compile time; late binding is at run time and is also called dynamic binding.","An abstract class (with a pure virtual function) cannot be used to make an object."],"x":["Polymorphism gives one name many forms. Compile time polymorphism is function overloading and operator overloading; the call is joined to the code at compile time (early or static binding).","Run time polymorphism uses virtual functions. A function in the base class is declared with the keyword virtual and the derived class redefines it. When the function is called through a base class pointer, the program decides at run time which version to run, from the type of the object the pointer points to. This is late (dynamic) binding.","Rules for virtual functions: they are members of a class (not static, not friend); they are called through a base class pointer; the prototype is the same in the base and derived classes; a class cannot have a virtual constructor but can have a virtual destructor. A pure virtual function is written virtual void show() = 0; it has no body, and a class that has one is an abstract class, from which no object can be made."],"e":"Base class Shape has a virtual function draw(). Circle and Square redefine it. A Shape pointer that points to a Circle calls the Circle version of draw()."},"9":{"s":"A file keeps data permanently on disk. C++ reads and writes files through streams of the fstream header: ifstream reads, ofstream writes and fstream does both. A file is opened with open() or by the constructor, and closed with close(). File modes include in, out, app, ate, binary and trunc. put() and get() handle characters; write() and read() handle binary blocks and whole objects. seekg and seekp move the file pointer for random access.","w":["ios::out erases the old contents of the file; ios::app adds at the end.","Binary files use read and write with a cast to a character pointer and the size of the object."],"x":["A file is a named collection of data kept on disk, so it stays after the program ends. A stream is a flow of data: the input stream brings data into the program and the output stream takes data out. The classes for file streams are ifstream (input), ofstream (output) and fstream (both), all in the header file fstream.","To use a file: make a stream object, open the file (with the open function or the constructor, giving the name and the mode), do the reading or writing, and close it. Modes: ios::in reads, ios::out writes, ios::app appends at the end, ios::ate goes to the end on opening, ios::trunc empties the file, ios::binary opens a binary file; modes can be joined with the vertical bar.","Sequential access reads or writes from the start to the end in order, with put() and get() for characters, or the operators and getline for text. For objects, a binary file is used: f.write((char *) &obj, sizeof(obj)) writes an object and f.read does the opposite. Random access moves the file pointer: seekg(n) for reading and seekp(n) for writing, with tellg() and tellp() giving the position. Command line arguments reach main as argc (the count) and argv (the strings)."],"e":"To store a student object: open an ofstream in binary mode, call write with the address and size of the object, and close the file. To read it back, open an ifstream and call read."}};
